entry.summary:
Ladies and gentlemen, we continue digging into the intricacies of web components. I made a bench here — comparing frameworks ($mol/Lit/Symbiote) on TodoMVC. Seems like we're talking about one thing, but the bench is about something else, right? Nope — to understand web components you need frameworks that put them front and center, the ones that "bet on them."
Here's what I managed to figure out:
First. Memory: 124 bytes per web component, and 16 bytes per JS object. An order of magnitude difference — that's a lot, and without virtualization the interface will most likely lag.
// Lit: each todo-item is an HTMLElement (C++ heap, ~124 bytes minimum)
@customElement("todo-item")
export class TodoItem extends LitElement { ... }
// <todo-item> immediately allocates a DOM node on createElement

//…
